e  that  there might be an attack because there's  so many of them you know odds are that  it'll be somebody else who gets caught  they're also very intelligent and birds  are able to quickly identify and ignore  things like scary eye balloons and  effigies  as well as startling noises this is one  reason why  propane cannons have a limited  effectiveness is that just like we tune  out a constant background noise  the birds will start to tune it out  so the challenge  given all of this  is how do we repel birds from a large  area without physically traversing the  area and you could  hire someone to drive around your corn  fields all day with an atv and i've  heard of farmers doing that  but of course that gets very expensive  and it requires that  you give up land for the atv to drive on  so that it's not crushing the crop  and this is a problem not only in  agriculture it's also a problem in  situations such as airfields where you  don't necessarily want someone  rushing around the airfield all the time  with you know in a vehicle  but you need to keep the birds  away from the planes that are taking off  or landing  so the solution to this  which was actually come up with quite a  while ago  was to use light  and in particular highly focused light  which is what we call lasers  the advantage of light  is  that it can  reach a very long distance and of course  it doesn't  um  pose any sort of physical resistance so  it can move  right past things right through a  cropped canopy without causing any  injury  for this reason lasers have been  investigated for bird control since  really the early days of laser  technology the first researchers working  on this were in the 1970s  those researchers at the time  were limited by the state of the laser  technology in those days  they were using gas lasers which were  very fragile and also required an  extremely large amount of power  and that limited their use they also  didn't really in those days have any way  to automate the lasers  since then  improvements in both laser diode  technology and battery technology have  removed most of these limitations  and you're probably all familiar with  handheld laser pointers  which take advantage of both of these  in the 1990s  we started seeing lasers adopted by  airports and industrial sites primarily  in europe  these were mostly hand-held human  operated units they often look like  large flashlights  they have a greatly expanded beam and  they're  powered by batteries  there were also some stationary  automated units that would turn on in  response to stimuli but didn't move  with these units the emphasis was on  covering large areas so the beam ranges  were up to  um  8 000 feet or more and these handheld  devices are very much still on the  market  the limitation with them  is of course that you have to have a  human operator at all times  and so that makes the most useful for  situations such as airports  where  the um  risk of monetary  damage from a bird strike incident is so  much greater than the cost of having an  employee  you know patrolling  with a handheld laser that it makes  financial sense  since 2010  there have been further improvements  both in laser technology but even more  importantly in  microcomputer technology and in solar  panel photovoltaic panel  technology  that have made solar  autonomous laser scarecrows possible  and reasonably affordable  unfortunately  lasers  since they do pose a risk of harm  both  harm to eyesight and  for higher power lasers a risk of burns  or starting fires  lasers are very strictly regulated  and a lot of the regulations around  lasers are quite old  and so it creates a very complex  regulatory environment  and this has discouraged  commercialization  of  mid-range models so right now if you  look um  at the commercial landscape you have  very low power models  designed for consumer use  and then you have  expensive high-end models that have very  elaborate  um safety  programming and things like that  now early research with lasers through  the 90s and the early part of this  century assumed that lasers would only  be effective under low light conditions  and so most of the studies  targeted roosting birds in the evening  and  most of these studies were not very  effective at moving birds away from  their roosting sites  they could disturb the birds but  oftentimes the birds would come back  and certainly once they stopped treating  the roosting site  with  a laser the birds would come back  however more recent studies including  our work  has focused on daytime use targeting  feeding  birds and has been much more successful  particularly when the laser  is automated and able to operate  continuously  during the daytime  so this is just a clip taken at  late dusk  that shows you what the laser beam looks  like as it moves through a  cornfield and i just want to note that i  have slowed this down to one quarter of  its actual speed so that you can see it  um one advantage of the laser is that it  can cover an area pretty quickly  so as you saw in that um  you know the beam is basically moving  through the tassels on the corn  we can only see the laser beam  in low light conditions like this but  the birds because they have so much  better eyesight than we do can see the  laser  even when the sun is shining  so i just want to talk a little bit  about some of the questions that we  frequently get asked  um first of all why are birds scared of  lasers we don't actually know  because of course we can't get into the  mind of the bird  but people who study bird behavior  have suggested that birds may perceive  the beam as a solid object  and so they move  in order to avoid that object  and it is true that in corn what we have  seen is that the primary effectiveness  of the laser  is that it stops the birds from perching  on the tassels  which they usually do before they move  down into the canopy  to feed also  by stopping perching on the tassels  it will  cause the birds that are keeping watch  for all of their flock mates  to move and that will result in the  entire flock moving  many people ask if birds become  acclimated to the lasers this is a  really good question because of course  that is the failure of most bird scare  technology  is that the birds become acclimated to  it over time  and we have some colleagues at the  university of florida  who have  been testing  exactly this question  using captive  european starlings in an outdoor aviary  and what they have found  is that the birds do not appear to  become acclimated to the lasers  and they may actually become more  sensitive more afraid of the laser with  repeated exposure  this study is ongoing we have one season  worth of data at this point and are  going to start a second season within  about a month  so hopefully  by later this year we'll have  an even more conclusive answer to this  but it does appear  that birds do not become acclimated to  the lasers  another question we often get is what  birds are controlled by lasers and  obviously  not every species of bird has been  tested  in general lasers seem to work best on  birds that flock together rather than  moving about  as individuals or just a few of them  there have been research studies that  have successfully used lasers on geese  cormorants gulls blackbirds robins cedar  wax swings  starlings and cowbirds  and there are other studies  currently underway  you know lots of different species are  being  controlled using lasers  so i want to talk just a little bit  about um using lasers specifically in  different crops  so  for any crop  you need to start laser protection  before the birds begin feeding in the  crop  with sweet corn this means  starting before the corn is mature  before the birds begin moving into the  field  and checking the corn out  in sweet corn you want to mount the  laser at tassel height  with the beam horizontal and this field  this clip that julie was kind enough 