 in  line of sight  if you can't see something you can't hit  it with a laser beam  and birds will  sneak in under the beam if it's going  over the tops of the tassels  um they they'll just basically treat it  like a roof and duck in under that roof  and get into the canopy to cause damage  now as julie said most of our research  has actually been with sweet corn that  is what all of our grant funding has  focused on but we have done a little bit  of research on other crops we are  constantly seeking funding to do  research on  more crops  um and we have learned a little bit  about using lasers in crops other than  sweet corn  um one thing we've learned is that for  short crops whether it's seedling corn  that you're trying to protect from geese  and crows  or turf grass sod that you're trying to  protect from winter feeding by geese or  strawberries  any short crop  you actually want to mount the scarecrow  as high as practical you can see down  here  this is a commercial unit that  unfortunately is no longer available in  the us that we were testing  on a kentucky bluegrass sod farm and  we've got it mounted on top of a  traveling irrigation rig about 15 feet  off the ground  um and then you aim the beam down  and what you want to do is  set the vertical movement of the laser  so that you're sweeping the crop  from the base of the scarecrow to the  edge of the field  again you need to be careful with a  short crop that the beam doesn't go into  a roadway or a neighbor's windows  that you keep it to your field  but that does seem to be the most  effective way  to try and keep particularly geese out  of fields  for blueberries on the other hand you  want to mount the scarecrow about three  feet or one meter above the canopy  and then move the beam  through the entire vertical range of  motion as well as horizontally  so that you're covering as many  different points in the canopy as  possible  what little we've learned about using  laser scarecrows in grapes it seems like  the approach you take is going to depend  on your training system  if all of  the clusters are basically at the same  level then you can treat grapes like  corn  and use the laser to create a virtual  lid over the field  but if the clusters are at all different  heights  then probably it needs to be dealt with  more like blueberries  where you're moving the beam through  many different places in the canopy  okay i want to close here  just by mentioning  sources of laser scarecrows  um there is currently only um  one real source of  um commercial  lasers that are designed for use in  agriculture and  other serious  outdoor bird control settings  and that is the avix autonomic from bird  control group these are sold all over  the world  um  the company itself is in the netherlands  and then they have a north american  office out in oregon  it is a commercial product from a  full-service company they will even come  and install your unit for you they  provide  on-site tech support a lot of different  things  these are 500 milliwatt lasers that can  cover very large areas depending on the  crop they a single unit can cover as  much as 100 acres  and they are designed to operate 24  hours a day year round  even in the winter with extremely low  temperatures  but of course  this is all going to cost you and the  list price of these units is about ten  thousand dollars  um so they're they're a good  unit but they're a very high-end unit  um  up next david is going to talk about the  uri laser scare pro which is the one  that we've been using in all of our  research  this is an  experimental  unit it's an open source  unit the design is available on our  website  we use 50 milliwatt lasers and we're  only able to cover up to nine acres but  we are deliberately targeting  the um  grower with smaller fields or with um  fields with lots of different  elevations  where you're not going to get the  economy of scale  that justifies using the larger  lasers our units will 