y into one of these beams  you could have  retinal damage  and  certainly repeated eye exposure over  time  is going to cause things like cataracts  and other  cumulative damage  so you don't want to look directly into  the beam  that said these beams  if the  is operating normally  are constantly moving and so that  combined with your blink reflex  really lowers the likelihood of actually  getting a beam directly in the eye for  long enough to cause damage  but still it's best to err on the side  of safety  okay great um  so somebody said what is the hazard  distance  um  we haven't actually measured it and i  would have to look it up for these  specific  lasers  um i don't know if dave remembers off  the top of his head what it is for these  yeah we did calculate this um near the  end of the season  we realized that was an omission from  the initial user manual and based on the  output power beam diameter and  divergence using the ansi  z136.1 calculation  we came up with a nominal ocular hazard  distance of 16 meters or 53 feet  so when you're outside that range  you should not be adversely affected  even if you did  happen to look at the beam within that  range  i would suggest you keep your head down  and walk over to the switch and turn it  off  so  all right so that sounds good i think  that uh it's time to uh for rebecca to  stop sharing her screen and then david  or if that's what what you're doing and  then david could get set up um  about his there was uh for his  presentation rebecca real quickly there  was another question about if you had  tested the lasers  in sweet cherry orchards at all  we have not tested them in sweet cherry  orchards  that would be  a very intriguing study to do we don't  have any sweet  cherry orchards in rhode island  um i know that there have been reports  of lasers being used in sweet cherry  orchards um i think it was in british  columbia  but again this is a fairly new um  technology in terms of being used in  agriculture a